"EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16523)\nciteturn90academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Title: EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents ... We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution.\nTitle: EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\nAuthors: Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\nDate: Mon Oct 12 10:11:38 2026\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.16523] EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents (https://borowiecki.dev/abs/2610.16523)\nciteturn90search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; View a PDF of the paper titled EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents, by Min Cai and Dan Zhang and Gao Xing and Libin Ma and Zhaohui Xu and Xi Ye ... We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution.\n\n# Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\n\n[Submitted on 12 Oct 2026]\n\n# Title:EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nAuthors:Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents, by Min Cai and Dan Zhang and Gao Xing and Libin Ma and Zhaohui Xu and Xi Ye\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Training LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\nSubjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2610.16523 [cs.AI]\n   | (or arXiv:2610.16523v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.16523 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Min Cai [view email]\n[v1] Mon, 12 Oct 2026 10:11:38 UTC (1,774 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMuse: Meta's personal AI agent, features & capabilities (https://ai.meta.com/muse/)\nciteturn90search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Your personal AI agent that gets things done ... Eligible purchases are covered by Link’s purchase protections, a first for AI agents, and your conversations are not shared with Meta's ad systems. ... Muse is built with automated protections, agentic AI security standards, and user controls designed to prevent harmful content and unauthorized actions.\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\nImage\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\nScroll to explore\n\n## Take the busywork off your plate\n\nMuse gets to know you and your goals, working across all aspects of life, so you can focus on the things that matter. It comes with strong privacy and security built in, so you stay in control.\n\n\n### A secure computer with its own browser\n\nMuse Secure VM runs on a persistent, dedicated virtual machine equipped with a state-of-the-art browser to navigate the web. It books appointments, fills out forms and handles customer service.\n\nMuse Secure VM runs on a persistent, dedicated virtual machine equipped with a state-of-the-art browser to navigate the web.\nIt books appointments, fills out forms and handles customer service.\n\n\n### Designed to feel like a conversation\n\nChat with your Muse in the same way you message with other people, using the Muse app or directly in WhatsApp. It’s simple, just tell Muse what you want to get done.\n\nImage: Muse asking for approval to purchase something you were looking for\n\n### Approve critical actions from your agent\n\nReview and approve actions from Muse before they happen, like sending emails and making purchases. See a complete audit trail of everything your agent has done (and what it is planning to do).\n\n### Your personal data is safe and secure\n\nYour logins are kept in a secure credential store your agent can't read, with 1Password integration coming soon. When shopping, a one-time card number is generated at checkout so your real card is never seen by the merchant or your agent. Eligible purchases are covered by Link’s purchase protections, a first for AI agents, and your conversations are not shared with Meta's ad systems.\n\nImage: Goals and ideas tracker\n\n### Always working on your goals and suggesting ideas\n\nGive Muse a goal or a task and it builds an action plan, tracks your progress and proactively takes tasks off your plate along the way. It learns and gets sharper, coming back with ideas and suggestions built around what you need, ready to act on them under your direction.\n\n\n### Works with your apps and builds its own tools\n\nConnect your email, calendar, Instagram and more apps you use daily. If a task needs a tool that doesn't exist, Muse builds it for you.\n\n## PRODUCT WALKTHROUGH\n\n### Get the most out of Muse\n\nWatch how to hand off more to your agent, connect what matters and keep progress moving.\n\nTry Muse\n\n0:00 / 0:00\n\n\n## TRY MUSE\n\n### Ready to get more done with Muse?\n\nGet started\n\n## FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS\n\n* * *\n\n### What can Muse do?\n\n  * Muse can answer questions, complete tasks, browse the web, make purchases, generate images, create documents, and connect with your favorite apps and services via Connectors. It can also set reminders, track goals, and monitor things you care about, all working in the background on your behalf.\n\nYes. When you ask Muse to do something, it keeps working in the background whether you're actively using the app or not. For example, if you ask it to monitor daily weather reports and alert you about rain, it will do that on its own without you needing to check in.\n\n* * *\n\n### Does Muse ask before it acts on my behalf?\n\nYes. Muse is designed to ask your permission before taking certain actions, like sending messages, making purchases, or sharing information with a connected app. You can choose to allow an action once, always allow it, or deny it. You can manage your default permission settings for every connection at any time.\n\n* * *\n\n### What is a personal AI agent?\n\nA personal AI agent is an AI that can take actions and get things done on your behalf, not just answer questions. Muse, for example, can browse the web, connect to your apps, complete multi-step tasks, and keep working in the background with your direction and oversight.\n\n* * *\n\n### How is a personal agent different from a chatbot or AI assistant?\n\nA chatbot responds to one question at a time. An AI assistant can handle more complex conversations and generate content. A personal AI agent like Muse goes further: it can take real actions across the web and connected apps, work through multi-step tasks on its own, and keep running in the background even after you close the app.\n\n* * *\n\n### What can a personal AI agent help with?\n\nA personal AI agent can help with everyday tasks like booking reservations, monitoring prices, managing reminders, creating documents, generating images, and researching topics. It can also connect to third-party apps and services to take actions on your behalf, like sending emails or completing online purchases. See more agentic AI examples.\n\n\n## Explore Meta AI products\n\nImage: Meta AI personal assistant on mobile device\n\n### Meta AI Assistant\n\n#### Get more done with Meta AI, your personal AI assistant.\n\nTry Meta AI\n\nLearn more\n\nImage: Meta AI media generation output cards\n\n### Image Generation\n\n#### Explore fun presets, make precise edits and visualize your ideas.\n\nStart generating--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBe There for Every Customer With Meta Business Agent (https://about.fb.com/news/2026/06/meta-business-agent?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_x-social-details_comments-action_comment-text)\nciteturn90search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago;   * We’re introducing Meta Business Agent, which lets businesses of all sizes increase their output and deliver personalized experiences for customers using AI. ...   * We’re also introducing the Meta Business Agent Platform, which gives businesses the infrastructure to build, customize, and deploy their Business Agents at scale.\n\nBack to Newsroom\n\nMeta\n\n# Be There for Every Customer With Meta Business Agent\n\nJune 3, 2026June 3, 2026\n\nListen to Article 10 Pause 10\n\n[Select]\n\nImage\n\n## Takeaways\n\n  * We’re introducing Meta Business Agent, which lets businesses of all sizes increase their output and deliver personalized experiences for customers using AI.\n  * Business Agent also doubles as a partner to deliver a morning briefing to catch you up on chats you missed overnight and provide insights on your threads.\n  * We’re also introducing the Meta Business Agent Platform, which gives businesses the infrastructure to build, customize, and deploy their Business Agents at scale.\n\nToday, we’re introducing Meta Business Agent – AI that lets every business show up for every customer as if they had an infinite team behind them. Business Agent can be set up in minutes or plugged directly into your existing enterprise infrastructure so you can 10X or 100X output.\n\n \n\nMore than one million businesses are already using a Meta Business Agent on WhatsApp and Messenger to respond to customers around the clock. And because there are more than one billion active threads with businesses on WhatsApp, Messenger and Instagram every day, a Business Agent can deliver more relevant, personalized experiences from the very first interaction. (Updated on June 3, 2026 at 1:40PM PT to reflect the latest business conversation stats)\nWe’re now expanding our Business Agent to businesses of all sizes globally, so you can have yours up and running within minutes, responding in your customers’ local languages using your tone. Your Business Agent can:\n\n  * Answer questions specific to your business\n  * Make product recommendations from a business catalog\n  * Book appointments and qualify incoming leads\n  * Let you decide when a team member steps in to provide support\n  * Close sales\n\nWe’re also expanding Business Agent to Instagram. Businesses can activate their Business Agent here, and getting started is free. In the coming months, businesses will access the agent through paid subscription offerings, with options for businesses of every size.\n\n## An AI Agent That Works for You\n\nBecause the Meta Business Agent responds to customers, it doubles as a partner that can deliver a morning briefing to catch you up on chats you missed overnight and provide insights on your threads. We’re starting with a select number of businesses on the WhatsApp Business app, Instagram Pro, Messenger, and Meta Business Suite, and in the future we’ll expand its capabilities to help fully run all your daily operations — like conducting market research, surfacing product insights, connecting with the tools to manage your calendar and providing competitive intelligence. You can join the waitlist here.\n\n## Personalizing Customer Experiences\n\nWe’re also introducing the Meta Business Agent Platform: a new agentic platform that gives businesses the infrastructure to build, customize, and deploy their Business Agent at scale. It lets businesses connect to a growing suite of hundreds of systems like Shopify, Zendesk, and Shopee giving Business Agents the ability to take action on behalf of the business. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmeta-agent/README.md at main · canvas-org/meta-agent · GitHub (https://github.com/canvas-org/meta-agent/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn90search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago;             messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": str(ctx.task)}], ... For detailed experiments, see Meta-Agent: Continual Learning for Agents. ...     @software{sleiman_meta_agent_2026,\n\n# Meta-Agent\n\nContinual learning for agents, without weight updates.\n\nMeta-Agent turns execution traces into persistent improvements to the system around a language model. It learns at two timescales:\n\n  * a fast loop improves the task-facing harness, including prompts, tools, hooks, stopping criteria, subagents, and control flow;\n  * an experimental slow loop improves the proposer skill that diagnoses failures and generates future harness changes.\n\nThe base model remains frozen. Meta-Agent keeps what it learns in harness code and versioned proposer instructions, backed by an on-disk experience store.\n\n> Research question: Can an agent improve both its harness and the process used to improve that harness?\n\nRead the technical report →\n\nMethod · Result · Quickstart\n\n## Overview\n\nAn agent is more than its model. The harness decides what context the model sees, which tools it can call, how it tracks state, when it checks its output, and when it stops.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgents API - Meta Agents Research Environments (https://facebookresearch.github.io/meta-agents-research-environments/api_reference/agents.html)\nciteturn90search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Agents in Meta Agents Research Environments are responsible for: ... The environment event reaction loop (handling user messages, notifications, and the overall agent lifecycle) is implemented in are/simulation/agents/default_agent/are_simulation_main.py. ...         def ask_user_help(agent):\n\n# Agents API¶\n\nAgents are the AI entities that interact with the Agents Research Environments to complete tasks. This section documents the core Agent API and related classes for creating and customizing agent behavior.\n\n## Overview¶\n\nAgents in Meta Agents Research Environments are responsible for:\n\n  * Task Execution: Understanding and completing assigned tasks\n\n  * Tool Usage: Interacting with apps through their exposed APIs\n\n  * Reasoning: Making decisions based on available information\n\n  * Learning: Adapting behavior based on feedback and results\n\nThe agent system is built around a ReAct (Reasoning + Acting) framework that allows agents to think, act, and observe in iterative cycles.\n\n## Creating Custom Agents¶\n\nAgents used on the CLI are built through are/simulation/agents/default_agent/agent_factory.py. The `BaseAgent` class describes the “inner loop” of the agent - the core reasoning and action execution logic. The environment event reaction loop (handling user messages, notifications, and the overall agent lifecycle) is implemented in are/simulation/agents/default_agent/are_simulation_main.py.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmeta-agents-research-environments/docs/foundations/index.rst at main · facebookresearch/meta-agents-research-environments · GitHub (https://github.com/facebookresearch/meta-agents-research-environments/blob/main/docs/foundations/index.rst?plain=true)\nciteturn90search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Meta Agents Research Environments is designed to address these gaps by offering strong foundations and abstractions that are general enough to support a wide range of tasks and benchmarks. ... - **Apps**: Similar to a desktop or mobile phone, the environment can support a set of apps that users use in their daily life, which the agent can interact with. ... In ARE, everything is an event, meaning any action the user, the agent takes or even external events generated by the environment itself, are events. ... Meta Agents Research Environments is built around fundamental concepts that work together to create dynamic, realistic simulations.\n\n* **Tool Usage**: Interacting with apps through their exposed APIs\n\n* **Reasoning**: Making decisions based on available information and context\n\n* **Learning**: Adapting behavior based on feedback and results\n\n* **Communication**: Sending messages to users and logging progress\n\n.. admonition:: Getting Started with Agents\n\nFor most use cases, we recommend using the default agent by specifying the ``--agent default``\n\nparameter when running CLI commands. This provides:\n\n* Robust ReAct loop implementation\n\n* Comprehensive logging and error handling\n\n* Flexible tool integration\n\n* Customizable system prompts and behavior\n\nThe default agent will automatically handle the event-based interactions with the environment, logging all actions and observations for later analysis.\n\nFor more details about the default agent implementation, see the ``ARE_react_json_agent`` function in ``ARE/agents/default_agent/agent_factory.py``.\n\nYou can also learn more about customizing agents in :doc:`../api_reference/agents` and explore practical examples in the tutorials.\n\n.. dropdown:: :octicon:`infinity` Universes\n\n:animate: fade-in\n\n**Universes** represent distinct, fully-populated instantiations of the simulated environment, each reflecting a specific user's digital world.\n\nThey provide the realistic foundation from which scenarios emerge, containing comprehensive synthetic data that mirrors authentic usage patterns.\n\n**Core Concept**\n\nUniverses embody a data-first approach to scenario development:\n\n* **Rich Environmental Foundation**: Each universe contains realistic application content including message histories, email threads, calendar events, and contacts\n\n* **Persona-Driven**: Built around detailed user personas that ensure coherence across all applications and data\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Zenera Platform | Zenera (https://zenera.ai/platform)\nciteturn90search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; At the center of Zenera sits the Meta-Agent - the user's single point of interaction for designing, building, and evolving agentic systems. ... The Meta-Agent decomposes the problem into specialized agents, defines their roles, selects appropriate models, and wires the entire system:\n\nPlatform\n\n# The Zenera Platform\n\nAn AI that builds AI systems - and proves they work before they run.\n\nTable of contents\n\n  * The Meta-Agent\n  * From Problem to Production\n  * Always-On: The Event-Reactive System\n  * Why Multi-Agent Systems Need a Meta-Agent\n  * Failure Modes of Human-Designed Agent Systems\n  * Why These Problems Scale Exponentially\n  * The Meta-Agent as Compiler, Linter, and Profiler\n  * Modification Safety: Change Without Fear\n  * Agent Certification\n  * Key Properties of Generated Systems\n\n## The Meta-Agent\n\nAt the center of Zenera sits the Meta-Agent - the user's single point of interaction for designing, building, and evolving agentic systems.\n\nThe Meta-Agent is not a chatbot. It is not a code generator. It is an AI system architect that translates business requirements into complete, verified, production-ready multi-agent systems.\n\n## From Problem to Production\n\n1\n\n### Describe the Problem\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApps - Meta Agents Research Environments (https://facebookresearch.github.io/meta-agents-research-environments/foundations/apps.html)\nciteturn90search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; A first env we introduce primarily aims at training agents capable of evolving in environments similar to those of operating systems such as iOS, Android, Meta RayBan OS, QuestOS, etc. ... The Agent-User Interface (AUI) app is a required component in every environment, serving as the communication bridge between users and AI agents.\n\nNon-exhaustive list of apps native to the Agents Research Environments\n\n  * Contacts\n\n  * EmailClient\n\n  * Messaging\n\n  * FileSystem\n\n  * Calendar\n\n  * Shopping\n\n  * Cab\n\n  * City\n\n  * ApartmentListing\n\nAmong these apps, a couple play a crucial role in the execution of the environment.\n\nSome apps in the initial environment play a crucial role in the execution of scenarios. We give more details below about the most important ones, giving a glimpse of the possibilities offered by the platform. Thanks to the high flexibility of the system, it is also possible to integrate MCP interfaces ( MCPApp - Model Context Protocol) and any other interface as long as they are wrapped to match the platform’s standards. For instance, some recent efforts have integrated SQL databases to test agents’ compatibility with different search strategies.\n\nAgent-User Interface\n\nThe Agent-User Interface (AUI) app is a required component in every environment, serving as the communication bridge between users and AI agents. Unlike traditional chat models where interaction happens through direct messaging, our system models user-agent communication through a dedicated ARE application.\n\nThis design requires the agent to make specific tool calls when reporting information to the user, rather than simply generating responses. When users send messages to the agent through the AUI, their content is automatically injected into the agent’s memory for processing. We highlight that this is the current implementation choice and that better alternatives may exist, like using the Notification System to surface user messages to the agent (see Notifications).\n\nThis tool-based communication model provides several advantages:\n\n  * It allows precise control over when the agent should communicate with the user versus when it should continue working silently on tasks\n\n  * It enables users to interact with the agent even while the agent is actively engaged in completing other objectives\n\nThe AUI thus creates a more realistic interaction pattern that mirrors how people actually work with digital assistants in complex, multi-tasking environments.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents | The Latest in AI (https://thelatestinai.com/papers/2610.16523)\nciteturn90search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; # EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents ... We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution.\n\ncs.AI·2610.16523 Copy arXiv ID·Oct 12, 2026\n\nSave\n\n# EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nAuthors: Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nOrganizations: University of Alberta · National University of Singapore · Kuaishou Technology · Independent Researcher · Alberta Machine Intelligence Institute (Amii)\n\n## Abstract\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Middle: Run 4 and 5 displayed in blue and red are two full runs. Dashed lines indicate online, intra-run modifications. Run 4 explored individual modifications through local perturbations, whereas Run 5 used endpoint interventions followed by replication, reversal, and interaction tests.†arxiv.org】View larger\n\nFigure 1: Illustration of how our meta-agent, EAU , manages RL training runs. EAU surpasses the prior SOTA on ColBench. Left: For the first three runs, EAU proactively restarts when it identifies issues that can only be resolved with offline patches. Middle: Run 4 and 5 displayed in blue and red are two full runs. Dashed lines indicate online, intra-run modifications. Run 4 explored individual modifications through local perturbations, whereas Run 5 used endpoint interventions followed by replication, reversal, and interaction tests.\n\nImage: Figure 2: Illustration of a user-centric agentic task. An agent needs to ask for clarifications about a user query.View larger\n\nFigure 2: Illustration of a user-centric agentic task. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv Troller (https://arxiv-troller.com/?q=paper%3A+2609.11636)\nciteturn90search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents ... We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution.\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nMin Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing et al.\n\narXiv:2610.16523\n\nAuthors: Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nSubmitted: Oct 2026 | Updated: Oct 2026\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUser-Centric Agent: Design & Evaluation (https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/user-centric-agent)\nciteturn90search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Unlike platform-centric or agent-centric agents, which optimize for system-driven objectives or agent behaviors, user-centric agents treat the user as both principal and partner: they align their policies to maximize utility as defined by user-specified goals, support proactive and adaptive interaction, and maintain transparency, privacy, and user autonomy as first-class principles (Eskenazi et al., 2019, Zhang et al., 17 Feb 2026).\n\n# User-Centric Agent: Design & Evaluation\n\nUpdated 25 February 2026\n\n  * User-centric agents are intelligent systems that prioritize evolving user needs and preferences, ensuring transparency, control, and user-defined satisfaction.\n  * They employ modular architectures with proactive intent recognition, dynamic context folding, and personalized memory augmentation to support adaptive interactions.\n  * Evaluation metrics focus on user-specific outcomes, such as success rates and personalization scores, while addressing challenges in scalability, privacy, and governance.\n\nA user-centric agent is an intelligent system whose design, operation, and success criteria are fundamentally anchored in the evolving needs, preferences, routines, and control of the user. Unlike platform-centric or agent-centric agents, which optimize for system-driven objectives or agent behaviors, user-centric agents treat the user as both principal and partner: they align their policies to maximize utility as defined by user-specified goals, support proactive and adaptive interaction, and maintain transparency, privacy, and user autonomy as first-class principles (Eskenazi et al., 2019, Zhang et al., 17 Feb 2026). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraphical User Interface - Meta Agents Research Environments (https://facebookresearch.github.io/meta-agents-research-environments/user_guide/understanding_ui.html)\nciteturn90search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Graphical User Interface¶ ... This guide is for the Meta Agents Research Environments (ARE) UI, explaining the full simulation tool interface, features and components. ... You can also view the agent logs following a conversation with the agent.\n\n# Graphical User Interface¶\n\n## Introduction¶\n\nThis guide is for the Meta Agents Research Environments (ARE) UI, explaining the full simulation tool interface, features and components. In the ARE UI, you will be able to run different models on a fixed baseline orchestration and environment, observe agent behavior, and explore Gaia2 environments and scenarios. Reference this guide to help you understand how to get started with ARE UI.\n\n## Getting Started¶\n\nUpon entering the ARE UI, you will be greeted by an introductory screen that provides an overview explanation of the different workspaces, along with links to documentation. There are two workspaces in the ARE UI: Playground and Scenarios. See below for details on each of these workspaces. The documentation resources will help you learn more about the ARE application itself as well as the Gaia2 benchmark. You can access this screen again anytime by clicking [about] at the bottom of the left navigation pane.\n\nClick [Got it] to proceed to the Scenario View\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMeta-Agent: From Task Descriptions to Verified Multi-Agent Systems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.25233)\nciteturn90academia13 [wordlim: 200] Publis